module

public import Sheaf.RingTheory.ExcellentRing
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Noetherian.Defs
import Mathlib.RingTheory.FiniteType

/-!
# Quotients of universally catenary Noetherian rings

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 00NK, lemma-quotient-catenary, second assertion
(blueprint: lem-per-universal-catenarity-quotient).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace IsUniversallyCatenary

/-- A quotient of a Noetherian universally catenary ring by any ideal is universally
catenary. Noetherianity is included in `IsUniversallyCatenary`.
Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 00NK, lemma-quotient-catenary, second assertion
(blueprint: lem-per-universal-catenarity-quotient).

Proof:
1. Let R = A/I with quotient map pi. For every ideal J of R, its inverse image K in A
   is finitely generated by Noetherianity (def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring). Images of its
   generators generate J: lift an element of J along pi and apply the generating
   expression in K. Hence R is Noetherian, as in Tag 00FN.
2. For any finite type R-algebra B, take finitely many algebra generators
   (def-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra). Restrict scalars along A -> R -> B. Lifting
   coefficients of polynomial expressions along pi shows that these same elements
   generate B over A. This is the finite-type case of Tag 0ECE applied to a quotient;
   I need not be prime or proper, and the list of generators can be empty.
3. Universal catenarity of A (def-exc-excellent-ring) makes this finite type A-algebra
   B catenary. The ring B and its prime chains are unchanged by restricting scalars.
   Thus every finite type R-algebra is catenary. Together with step 1 this is exactly
   universal catenarity of R, by Tag 00NL.
-/
theorem quotient (A : Type u) [CommRing A] (hA : IsUniversallyCatenary A) (I : Ideal A) :
    IsUniversallyCatenary (A ⧸ I) := by
  sorry

end IsUniversallyCatenary
